      "math_academy_id": "e-12875",
      "problem": "Find the value of $2 + \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} \\div \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right)^0.$",
      "worked_solution": "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nAny number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right) ^0 = 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n\\left(-4\\right)^{-2}  = \\dfrac{1}{(-4)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{(-4)}^{-2}÷{(1.5+\\frac{2}{15})}^{0} & = \\\\ 2+\\frac{1}{16}÷1 & = \\\\ 2+\\frac{1}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{32}{16}+\\frac{1}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{33}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
